    "contentAsciiDoc": "=== Thermionic Emission, Child-Langmuir Space Charge Law, and m-Resolution Field Dynamics\n\nIn vacuum electronic devices, electron charge transport is initiated by heating a conductive metallic or oxide-coated surface inside an evacuated envelope. Thermal kinetic energy transfers into electron wave packets on the discrete crystal lattice grid \\( \\mathcal{G}_N \\), enabling energetic carriers to overcome the metal work function barrier \\( \\Phi \\). Under Jaynes Maximum Entropy (MaxEnt) state partitioning subject to lattice energy bounds, the emission current density \\( J \\) is governed by the Richardson-Dushman thermionic equation:\n\\[ J = A^* T^2 e^{ - \\frac { \\Phi } { k_B T } } \\]\nwhere \\( A^* = \\frac { 4 \\pi q m_e k_B^2 } { h^3 } \\approx 120.4 \\text{ A/(cm}^2 \\text{K}^2) \\) is Richardson’s constant, \\( T \\) is absolute temperature in kelvins, \\( k_B \\) is Boltzmann’s constant, and \\( \\Phi \\) is the metal work function.\n\n==== Space-Charge Cloud Formation and the Child-Langmuir Law\nWhen emitted electrons leave the cathode surface, they accumulate in the vacuum gap, forming a negative space-charge cloud. This cloud creates a localized electrostatic potential well that repels subsequently emitted electrons back toward the cathode. The space-charge field is governed by the Master Field Equation \\( D F = J \\) on the multiscale grid \\( \\mathcal{G}_N \\). In one spatial dimension \\( x \\) between cathode (\\( x = 0 \\)) and anode (\\( x = d \\)), Poisson’s potential equation yields:\n\\[ \\frac { d^2 V } { d x^2 } = - \\frac { \\rho ( x ) } { \\varepsilon_0 } \\]\nwhere \\( \\rho ( x ) \\) is the space-charge density. Conservation of current requires \\( J = - \\rho ( x ) v ( x ) \\), where velocity \\( v ( x ) \\) is determined by energy conversion on \\( \\mathcal{G}_N \\):\n\\[ v ( x ) = \\sqrt { \\frac { 2 q V ( x ) } { m_e } } \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( \\rho ( x ) = - \\frac { J } { v ( x ) } \\) into Poisson’s equation yields the non-linear differential equation:\n\\[ \\frac { d^2 V } { d x^2 } = \\frac { J } { \\varepsilon_0 } \\sqrt { \\frac { m_e } { 2 q V ( x ) } } \\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\( 2 \\frac { d V } { d x } \\) and integrating from the cathode (where \\( V = 0 \\) and \\( \\frac { d V } { d x } = 0 \\) under space-charge limiting conditions) yields:\n\\[ \\left( \\frac { d V } { d x } \\right)^2 = \\frac { 4 J } { \\varepsilon_0 } \\sqrt { \\frac { m_e } { 2 q } } V^{1/2} \\implies \\frac { d V } { d x } = 2 \\left( \\frac { J } { \\varepsilon_0 } \\right)^{1/2} \\left( \\frac { m_e } { 2 q } \\right)^{1/4} V^{1/4} \\]\nIntegrating across the vacuum gap from \\( x = 0 \\) to \\( x = d \\) with anode potential \\( V ( d ) = V_P \\) gives:\n\\[ \\frac { 4 } { 3 } V_P^{3/4} = 2 \\left( \\frac { J } { \\varepsilon_0 } \\right)^{1/2} \\left( \\frac { m_e } { 2 q } \\right)^{1/4} d \\]\n\nSquaring both sides and isolating current density \\( J \\) establishes the fundamental **Child-Langmuir 3/2-Power Space-Charge Law**:\n\\[ J = \\frac { 4 } { 9 } \\varepsilon_0 \\sqrt { \\frac { 2 q } { m_e } } \\frac { V_P^{3/2} } { d^2 } \\]\nOn the discrete lattice grid \\( \\mathcal{G}_N \\), the spatial resolution \\( \\delta x \\) bounds the potential gradient near the cathode surface, eliminating non-physical continuum infinities while maintaining exact field flux conservation."
