    "contentAsciiDoc": "=== Formal Postulates and Theorems of Vacuum Tube Field Dynamics\n\n[#postulate-vacuum-field-transport]\n[POSTULATE]\n.Postulate: Vacuum Space-Charge Field Transport\n====\nInside an evacuated envelope on the discrete lattice grid \\( \\mathcal{G}_N \\), thermionic electron transport is governed by non-singular multivector field dynamics \\( D F = J \\), where space-charge field density \\( \\rho \\) and velocity \\( v \\) are bounded by discrete resolution limits \\( \\delta x \\) and \\( \\delta t \\).\n====\n\n[#theorem-child-langmuir-law]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Derivation of the Child-Langmuir Space-Charge Law\n====\nIn a planar vacuum diode with inter-electrode spacing \\( d \\) and applied anode potential \\( V_P \\), space-charge limited current density \\( J \\) satisfies:\n\\[ J = \\frac { 4 } { 9 } \\varepsilon_0 \\sqrt { \\frac { 2 q } { m_e } } \\frac { V_P^{3/2} } { d^2 } \\]\n\n*Proof:*\n\n1. **Poisson-Ampère Equation**: One-dimensional Poisson field relation on \\( \\mathcal{G}_N \\) gives \\( \\frac { d^2 V } { d x^2 } = - \\frac { \\rho ( x ) } { \\varepsilon_0 } \\).\n2. **Current Continuity and Conservation**: \\( J = - \\rho ( x ) v ( x ) \\) and energy conservation \\( \\frac { 1 } { 2 } m_e v ( x )^2 = q V ( x ) \\).\n3. **Differential Equation**: \\( \\frac { d^2 V } { d x^2 } = \\frac { J } { \\varepsilon_0 } \\sqrt { \\frac { m_e } { 2 q V ( x ) } } \\).\n4. **First Integration**: Multiplying by \\( 2 \\frac { d V } { d x } \\) yields \\( \\left( \\frac { d V } { d x } \\right)^2 = \\frac { 4 J } { \\varepsilon_0 } \\sqrt { \\frac { m_e } { 2 q } } V^{1/2} \\).\n5. **Second Integration**: Integrating from \\( x = 0 \\) to \\( x = d \\) with boundary conditions \\( V ( 0 ) = 0 \\) and \\( V ( d ) = V_P \\) gives \\( \\frac { 4 } { 3 } V_P^{3/4} = 2 \\left( \\frac { J } { \\varepsilon_0 } \\right)^{1/2} \\left( \\frac { m_e } { 2 q } \\right)^{1/4} d \\).\n6. **Final Result**: Squaring and isolating \\( J \\) yields \\( J = \\frac { 4 } { 9 } \\varepsilon_0 \\sqrt { \\frac { 2 q } { m_e } } \\frac { V_P^{3/2} } { d^2 } \\). \\( \\square \\)\n===="
